==Question #1==
{{Cquote2|What does ‘aspirational content’ mean to you? Who are these Awakened bosses targeted at, and what can we expect from them in terms of difficulty?}}

[https://youtu.be/9NrVrzYd7EM&t=367s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Arcane:''' This is obviously quite a big and philosophical one. For me, it's mainly having content that you strive to do! That might be many things: you want to get higher skills, to make some more money, to get better at the game - all these different things that the game provides you a means to be bad at, as well as good at in various regards to eventually be able to accomplish something.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I play Old School RuneScape right now because of the Inferno! Back then, when I was a lot younger, the
Fight Caves were really iconic, and it was really cool to go and get your Fire Cape. Then seeing that
they've done a sequel to that - which is some of my favourite content - I was like "I really need to do
that!"
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Therefore, when I first played my main, everything I did was about: How do I get to Inferno as quickly as possible? What are my stats? How do I make money? I remember killing thousands of Vorkaths, so that I could eventually get my Twisted Bow. That is why aspirational content should be that thing that truly motivates you to push, because you want to get it for whatever reason.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' As much as Infernal Cape is really cool and Best-in-Slot, it's only really for strength bonus; it doesn't really change your actual gameplay very much. However, it's just cool to go like "I defeated Inferno, I have this cape!" - it's such a prestigious thing.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' That's on the extreme end - aspirational content is all over the place as well. Even if it's just like "I want to get higher mining, so then I can do Volcanic Mine" - that can be aspirational content to some people. I think it's a lot easier to see true aspirational content when it's in the grand scheme like Inferno.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Who are these Awakened bosses targeted at? I've been seeing these next 4 bosses from Desert Treasure 2 as the next tier of bosses. God Wars is obviously aimed at a specific demographic, but it's quite old content and introduces tier 70 items - or not even tier 70; in some cases, tier 60-65. In other words, I'm seeing these DT2 bosses as the next content of PvM bosses, and the Awakened versions are even a level above that.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I'm hoping that the regular DT2 bosses prove to actually be quite a challenge and to people that are maybe just comfortable with killing Vorkath, the base bosses should actually be fairly aspirational themselves. These rings are going to be worth a lot of money like Best-in-Slot pieces of gear that you're using everywhere in the game. So I do see these bosses also as being aspirational in the first place.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' However, our player base is so good at the game now. As much as these might be aspirational to people that are just comfortable doing Vorkath etc., some players have done hundreds if not thousands of raids and actual hard pieces of content, and have got Infernal Cape, started speedrunning Inferno etc. It's just like "Can we just give them something a little bit over the top? Can we give something to challenge them?"
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I'm hoping that these Awakened bosses really target those players specifically that have been craving these challenges. As much as ToA had it with doing 600 invocation raids and such, I think it scratched the itch a bit for some of those people. There is obviously a back and forth in terms of it not being fun to just have higher and higher defence, higher and higher hit points, and that's completely valid. We
want to be able to introduce new mechanics as well.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' However, increasing stats is important, too. I know Chambers of Xeric got a lot of criticism on release - or even to this day - that just having 50% increase in defence and obviously all the other stats is just a bit of a boring way to go about it. It's not particularly interesting. I remember taking those learnings and that feedback into designing Hard Mode ToB, and we didn't change any stats in Hard Mode ToB - we just added additional mechanics and tried to mix it up.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Our players are already really good at ToB, so adding one or two additional mechanics to ToB didn't change it much, either. We actually got a lot of feedback then, saying we probably should have had a bit higher stats as well. Not saying we should go to the extremes as Chambers of Xeric - which is like 50% additional defence, it's just a ridiculous amount. But maybe they should have had higher hit points, hit a bit harder and have slightly higher defence, just to make it feel a bit beefier and to make it feel like the step up.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I'm hoping these Awakened ones feel like that middle ground, where they've got some additional mechanics to make the more mechanical challenge, but they also have additional stats. Because we expect them to be only one-off completions, it's a lot easier to design something that is a curated challenge for 1KC. That's a lot easier to design than making something that's really hard for 1KC, but is also fun after 50, 100, 200 and doesn't just become trivial - that's a lot harder.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' We want to make something that's a really good challenge, but we're not going to make this like a Hard Mode variant into a grindable variant that's efficient for all the uniques - because that ends up becoming the Meta. I do think they will have better drops, but I don't think they'll be efficient for grinding the uniques. I think that we want to have them as "You managed to kill this boss!" as opposed to "Oh yeah, I grind Awakened Leviathan every day". It's just a different type of approach to the same thing. Inferno was never designed to be grinded - it was very much a one-off completion. I don't think it's gonna sit on the level that Inferno was for your first time if you've not got Cape yet. I'm hoping it scratches that itch.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' Aspirational content is a relative term. For some people, the Fire Cape is still aspirational content. I think the wording that we used in the blog was quite deliberate, and I didn't want to write it's going to be as hard as in Inferno, because that's just a hard expectation to set. People are good at different things, and difficulty is subjective in Old School. But I feel that the people that really enjoy the Inferno, or early days of ToA doing 500s, or like doing GMs - the people that enjoy that kind of stuff will probably enjoy what we plan to do with these. If you can't do it, then that's fine; the regular bosses are there! The Orbs that we're proposing to give them an extra financial incentive (for players killing the regular post-quest variants).
* '''Mod Goblin:''' Making comparisons is a bit tricky - I made a mistake by writing that you'd probably be able to shred through Muspah quicker than Vorkath, which turned out not to be true. But I feel like it's not that controversial to say that these are probably harder than all of the other Grandmaster quest bosses, in my opinion. I think all four of them are the top four most difficult post-quest bosses by quite a bit.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I completely agree. I do think these bosses are a step up from the existing ones. At the end of the day, it's not going to be something you can't just out-gear and come in with a full inventory of brews and probably manage to scrape through. I think Sins of the Father and Vanstrom is really hard when you're designed to do the fight when you're like 70s, maybe low 80s combat. That fight is really difficult, but if you go in with Max gear and a full inventory brews, you're gonna be able to make so many mistakes - it doesn't really matter, right?
* '''Mod Arcane:''' That's where stats matter - how much damage you take matters. It's hard to make it perfect for every player at every piece of the game. We're releasing Grandmaster quests that someone who's done 50 Inferno KC and 500 Hard Mode ToB goes in and feels like this isn't a challenge. That player probably passed every Grandmaster quest in that regard, and that's a shame.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Mechanically, I do think these bosses all offer something a bit different. They're all fairly unique, and I do think you should struggle if you don't do them right. There will be a step up in expectations for gear, etc. You're probably not going to want to go in with an Iban's Blast and just start hitting everything - you're gonna have a bad time. For most of the quest bosses - even Song of the Elves - you can get away with Blood Barrage, you can get away with your Iban's Blast, and you don't really need that gear. And I think you'll have a really bad time if you don't, for these bosses.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' Oh no, magic confirmed!
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Oh my God, one of the three styles is used on one of the four bosses??? Damn!

==Question #2==
{{Cquote2|I love the ideas, but why are the Orbs tradeable? It feels quite odd; if the boss is going to be locked behind something, it shouldn't be buyable on the GE.}}

[https://youtu.be/9NrVrzYd7EM&t=984s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Arcane:''' I think it feels odd because it’s different. We’ve never really done this before - we’ve never really locked being able to do something behind a consumable object. The idea behind making it tradeable is that if a regular player is doing the regular bosses and either don’t have the gear or just aren’t interested and don’t want to do these bosses, they can just sell them.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' It can become a lucrative venture where high-end players who want to get their Awakened KC and have to do 5 to 10 attempts, there ends up being this economy loop where lower-level players are fueling high-level players, allowing the high-level players to spend millions on these Orbs and attempt it over and over again without having to grind the boss out. That would just be tedious.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' This way, low-level players benefit from it. I think that’s a really interesting economic incentive and is how most of our game works. How many regular mains are fishing for their Karambwans and their Manta Rays? Not very many - they’re benefitting from all the other people who are doing that content.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I know it’s slightly different for Ironmen. It’s going to be a lot harder, and you’ll have to go grind out your Orbs. I think this actually adds to the prestige of it, though. ToA is a good comparison for this.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' On ToA’s release, there was a bit of disappointment that players could complete both Regular and Expert Mode within the first couple of hours on the first day. It wasn’t the same as Inferno or ToB, which took multiple days. People want that chase of struggling and being unable to complete something for a while.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I think the main reason why Inferno and ToB took so long to complete is that if you failed that content, you’re set back a considerable amount of time thanks to the wiping system in ToB, where you have to do the entire raid again, or in Inferno, you have to go through all 60 waves again.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' If it wasn’t for that forced amount of time you had to put in to learn it, I don’t think it would have taken very long to clear at all if you could just jump to Zuk repeatedly. I don’t think Zuk’s fight is that hard; it’s just that you’ve spent two hours killing monsters and draining supplies, and then you get one attempt and accidentally mess up. Then you’ve got to do it all again. We’re basically gating your learning time via another mechanic in the rest of the raid or the rest of the waves.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' While this isn’t the same, I think it has somewhat of a similar effect. Because you need a certain amount of Orbs, people can't just spam these over and over and over again like they need to go kill the targeted bosses and need to wait till they actually enter the economy. Then it adds to that little bit of hype, that bit of a race where it's like, “Oh god damn, I really want as many orbs as possible. I want to be the one with the first Awakened KC?”. I think that adds a lot of excitement. I think the economy benefits because the entire loop will make all the bosses more lucrative for most players.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' It also adds to the day one, week one, hype of who can get the Awakened KC even more exciting because there will be a lot of forks in the economy; it just comes down to how much you are willing to pay. They're going to skyrocket very quickly if there's a lot of demand on day one.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I could just make all these bosses have 10 or 20 times the health and add 60 waves before them or something, and then as soon as you die, go do it all again. But I don't think we can really do that in a way that would feel very good or meaningful. So this is a different approach.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' One of the things that I quite like about it, conceptually, is that, even for mains, there are stakes - which you don't usually have in, for example, the Inferno, especially if it's your first cape. You use some supplies, but then they're relatively inexpensive. If you're at the point where you care about the Inferno, you probably don't care that much about the supply cost; you just pot over and over and over and over again and learn so much in a short time.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' It's cool in PvM, especially in a game like Old School in 2023, which is heavy on positive progression. There's not really that much risk unless you’re doing PvP. There are not really a lot of negative progression angles or anything like that or regression. I think it’s cool to add stakes like “I spent money on this” or “I spent time getting this”, and this is my one-shot, and if I mess it up, then I gotta get another one. I think that's exciting, and that makes it interesting, as opposed to just dying and running back in and learning everything instantly.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I completely agree. I think it has so much potential to add interesting dynamics to encounters like this. At the end of the day, if it comes in the game, and you guys try it out, you don't like it, and it's just annoying, and you don't really care for the benefits, then we just won't do it in the future. We're trying something new, and we think it might be really cool.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' Yeah, it's nice to be able to experiment with stuff, right? It's cool to try new systems and see what sticks. Whether people like it or not, you can always take away learnings. Whether that's things like this or the invocation system from Raids 3, there are always bits we can plug in and take out or mess with and improve on in the future.

==Question #3==
{{Cquote2|Why are these harder versions not efficient for uniques? I think it would be awesome if a rare Orb drop from the normal version of the boss translated to a very high unique chance for those players willing to fight the significantly harder variant.}}

[https://youtu.be/9NrVrzYd7EM&t=1334s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Arcane:''' I covered this earlier, but just to be a bit more concise on it: As much as I completely sympathise or empathise with the idea of “Harder bosses should give better rewards”. Generally, our entire game translates this way; harder content, more rewards. I think this generally just comes down to the fact that we don't really have that much time to develop these.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' What I was leaning on earlier is that to develop something that is a really big challenge, a one-off completion that is also fun to grind after 100, 200, and 300 KC, is very difficult. Just creating something fun to grind is hard in itself. Even ignoring the one-time KC challenge, creating something that's both is a lot more difficult. If Inferno was designed with the intent of it being grindable, I don't think it would have been anything like it is today. I don't think having to do two-hour or one-hour attempts is probably fun for most people.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' If we look historically at some other pieces of content, you've got Phosani’s and regular Nightmare, and people doing original Nightmare solo a while ago wasn't particularly fun. Nightmare didn't really live up to that.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' I think Gauntlet is a more controversial one, some people love Gauntlet, and some people really dislike it. I don't really enjoy cryptic long-term. I can do like three or four KC in a night, and then I don't really want to do anymore. And I do think there are a lot of people in that boat.
* '''Mod Arcane:''' Making something that's fun to grind is really difficult. You guys just play this game so much. Most of the games don't have this problem - like Mythic Raids. As much as you repeatedly wipe against the same boss, you're moving on quickly in the grand scheme of things. In our game, though, you're killing Corporeal Beast for like 5000 KC - how do you make that fun long-term? Making it a non-grindable, meta thing for getting uniques and stuff means we don't have to worry about that as much. We can just lean in and really curate something that is a good challenge for one KC. And hopefully, that's more enjoyable. This basically sums it up.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' There’s also the element that for the people who will most be interested in being able to repeat the bosses, it's the same as doing Inferno a second time. It’s pointless bar like CAs, but people do it; people push it. Or people in the game with 42-minute Inferno times and stuff… I don't think this will be on the same level of complexity because it's not as long, and there's not as much opportunity for time-saving. There are a lot of emergent opportunities for people to have fun repeating them, get better, push them and solve them differently.
* '''Mod Goblin:''' I completely agree with the points that it is hard to make something fun to do, minute to minute, for hundreds of kills. Instead of fun on the promise of the eventual reward you're getting for having done the thing. It’s tricky.

